# Accessibility Guide

All Bali ViewComponents target **WCAG 2.1 Level AA** compliance.

## Core Principles

### 1. Perceivable

Users must be able to perceive all content.

- Text alternatives for images (`alt` attributes)
- Color contrast ratios (4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for large text)
- Don't rely on color alone to convey information

### 2. Operable

Users must be able to operate all interactive elements.

- All functionality available via keyboard
- No keyboard traps
- Sufficient time for interactions

### 3. Understandable

Users must be able to understand content and interface.

- Clear, consistent navigation
- Predictable behavior
- Error identification and suggestions

### 4. Robust

Content must work with assistive technologies.

- Valid HTML markup
- Proper ARIA usage
- Name, role, value programmatically determinable

---

## Interactive Elements

All buttons, links, and controls must have:

| Requirement | Implementation |
|-------------|----------------|
| Keyboard accessible | Native focusable element or `tabindex="0"` |
| Focus visible | `:focus-visible` styles (DaisyUI provides this) |
| Role announced | Native element or `role` attribute |
| Name announced | Visible text, `aria-label`, or `aria-labelledby` |
| State announced | `aria-expanded`, `aria-selected`, `aria-pressed` |

---

## Common Patterns

### Buttons

```erb
<%%# GOOD - Native button with visible text %>
<%%= render Bali::Button::Component.new(name: 'Save Changes') %>

<%%# GOOD - Icon button with aria-label %>
<%%= render Bali::Button::Component.new(
  icon: 'x',
  'aria-label': 'Close dialog'
) %>
```

### Forms

```erb
<%%# Labels are automatically associated %>
<%%= f.bali_text_field :email,
  label: 'Email',
  hint: "We'll never share your email",
  error: @user.errors[:email].first
%>
```

### Modals

Bali's Modal component automatically includes:
- `role="dialog"`
- `aria-modal="true"`
- Focus trap within modal
- Return focus on close
- Escape key handling

